phase P4
title Preview
description
    **Class:** not readily verifiable.

    **Kind:** verification-suite.

    **Goal:** the preview cut the user named (2026-11-12): the fixtures, including Pay and checkout, in real Chrome and
    Safari through a minimal React binding and IndexedDB; the rest of the core's slices (fragments, reads and
    operations, release versions); `flow check`; and `nub run verify` building v3.

    **Starts when:** P3's completion audit passes.

    **Verified by:**

    - a real-browser run of the fixtures, whose driver and checks @task.V3.T58 builds and proves first; it runs alone;
    - for the core slices, the same suite as P3: Story tests, run rules and compile-time tests from each Spec, with a
      planted fault for each new rule in code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/faults.ts;
    - skill:check-task per Task; skill:check-runtime in the real browsers; `nub run check` at zero.

    **One iteration:** the next ready Task: its Spec change, its requirements as failing checks, the code, an
    independent skill:check-task. The user's page is written at each slice's close.

    **Exit criteria:**

    - every Task is done;
    - the fixtures run in real Chrome and Safari, and a fault planted in the browser path is caught there;
    - `nub run verify` passes and builds `packages/flow-state-v3`;
    - the suite's coverage record is updated, and an audit by a lead that did not build the suite has
      passed (skill:policy-testing#audit-a-suite);
    - the completion audit names each Plan outcome these Tasks satisfy and finds it delivered.

    **False pass:** a browser run that passes on a fake store or a fake page.

    **Caught by:** skill:check-runtime on the real interface, and a planted fault in the IndexedDB path.

    **Gates:**

    - `nub run faults`, which rejects the dead core and the named faults in code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/faults.ts;
    - the browser run, which rejects its own planted fault (built by @task.V3.T58);
    - `nub run verify`.

    **Failure limit:** 2.

    **Stops:**

    - a guarantee that can't be met;
    - public entry-point names or a large public API change.

    **User's read:** each Spec revision; small public API calls; the user's page at each slice's close.

    **Draws from:**

    - skill:policy-verification and skill:policy-testing, as in P3;
    - skill:check-runtime for the browser run;
    - skill:to-spec and skill:to-design per module; skill:execute-task and skill:check-task per Task;
    - skill:review at a Task's first commit and its close.

    **Order:** @task.V3.T92, @task.V3.T95, @task.V3.T96, @task.V3.T58, @task.V3.T57, @task.V3.T77, @task.V3.T76,
    @task.V3.T72, @task.V3.T78, @task.V3.T21, @task.V3.T73, @task.V3.T56, @task.V3.T22, @task.V3.T23, @task.V3.T97
    (re-plan section 8, the user, 2026-11-18). A `blocked_by` link is a real dependency only; where several Tasks are
    ready, take the earliest in this order.

    Every Task below is a draft. Before starting its lead, the orchestrator writes its full text with skill:to-task. The
    earlier text of each, in `wiki/tasks/V3/` at commit `6b9fe478`, was written for the conformance suite: it is
    background only, and its acceptance does not carry over. The suite's coverage record is the one @task.V3.T67 starts;
    the browser run adds its own entry to it.
tasks
    T58
        title       Browser slice: the fixtures in real browsers with minimal React and IndexedDB
        status      draft
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@task.V3.T9]
        description
            Run the fixtures, including Pay and checkout, in real Chrome and Safari on production, through a minimal
            React binding and IndexedDB persistence.

            **Why now:** it is the browser half of the preview cut (the user, 2026-11-12), and until it runs, the fake
            store and fake page that every other test uses are unchecked against the real thing.

            **Read first:** @plan.Persistence.O1, @plan.React.O1, @plan.Pending.D9 (`Store` and `storeContract`),
            @task.V3.T19 (write-ahead, checked on `Fake.storage` until this Task) and @task.V3.T28 (completes the
            adapters), the Safari findings in `packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/safari/RESULTS.md`, and the prototype
            adapter code:packages/flow-state-v3/prototypes/round-14/reality/idb.ts (reference only; the production
            adapter is written fresh).

            **Approach:** `Store` becomes its own port in `src/host/store.ts`: a Context service with a Schema'd error
            vocabulary. This Task owns the store contract test, a plain function of a `Store` Layer under
            `@effect/vitest` (`storeContract`, D9). Its IndexedDB adapter is minimal but already meets Safari's D1 to
            D4, since the fixtures run in Safari here; @task.V3.T28 completes it.

            May change: `src/host/`, `fixtures/`, `tests/`, and a new browser-run folder. Evidence: this
            Task's comments.

            False pass: a run that never touches real IndexedDB → caught by a planted fault in the store path that only
            the real store shows.
        acceptance
            - The fixtures run in real Chrome and Safari, each alone, and the run fails on a fault planted in the
              IndexedDB path.
            - The store contract test this Task writes passes against `Fake.storage` and against real IndexedDB in real
              Chrome and Safari, and the same is shown for the fake page's timers and lifecycle against a real page.
            - The React binding is the least that renders the fixtures; @task.V3.T30 builds the rest.
            - Fixtures first (code:wiki/conventions/fixtures.md): step 9 of code:wiki/conventions/fixture-catalog.md:
              every fixture run with no new feature, as the step says; each scenario red before the build, and the
              step's cells marked used at the close.
        comment     2026-11-16T09:26:06Z
            Routed from T19: the flush at once on visibilitychange to hidden and on pagehide (WriteAhead R10, I12, T5)
            has no check: the edge's hide path exists, but the browser platform and the Fake host stub hidden and
            onHidden. Owed here with the real page: a Fake page word for hide, a Story over it, and planted faults for a
            hide that waits or does nothing.
        comment     2026-11-16T09:45:52Z
            Routed from T19's close review: on a hidden page a refused batch is issued again at once
            (code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/host/edge.ts#makeEdge pump ignores due when hidden), so a refusing store is
            retried in a loop against the 5 s rule; and a refused batch's put-back records can be stale when the store
            holds then refuses, which the Fake cannot do yet. Check both with the store contract and the
            visibility flush.
        comment     2026-11-16T13:43:18Z
            Routed from T9's close review: getSnapshot builds a new object on every read and outside() copies memory for
            machines with private fields, which breaks useSyncExternalStore's same-value contract; cache the snapshot
            per published turn.
        comment     2026-11-17T15:28:53Z
            Owed from T85 (b6090c05): time the hide handler in real Chromium and Safari (Node: about 6 us per changed
            actor, 6.2 ms for 1,000) before any idle pre-encoding.
