    T76
        title        Declared props on local actors
        status       draft
        priority     high
        blocked_by   [@task.V3.T9, @T77]
        derives_from [@plan.Machines.O1]
        satisfies    [
                     @plan.Machines.O1,
                     @plan.Machines.O4,
                     @plan.Machines.O7,
                     @plan.Machines.O8,
                     @plan.Runtime.O3,
                     @plan.Runtime.O4
                     ]
        description
            Build declared `props` and `onProps` on local actors, with `createActor`'s props and `Story.machine`'s
            `props(values)` step.

            **Why now:** configurable widgets mirror parent props into memory through a `Configure` event (the number
            input fixture does); the user accepted declared props instead (decisions Q5, §9 R1 to R3). Starts after the
            Plan merge.

            **Read first:** @spec.Turn.R28, @plan.Machines.O7, code:wiki/conventions/where-a-value-lives.md,
            @task.V3.T9, code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/ui-primitives-decisions-2026-11-16.md (§9).

            **Risk:** `Story.machine` is @task.V3.T29's; this Task builds its first slice (input, props and the props
            step) and T29 extends it (U3, decided by the orchestrator for the user, 2026-11-16). Blocked by T9, which
            builds `createActor` and the mailbox, and by T77, because emit mappers read props. `createActor`'s options
            stay a record so `props` fits.

            **Approach:** definition `props` with Schema defaults; a props update is its own turn, changing only props,
            then `onProps`; every memory reader gets props from the same snapshot; construction rejects a prop that
            collides in the one namespace.

            May change: `src/`, `fixtures/`, `tests/`. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: props read only by guards → caught by tests that read them in `updateMemory`, an emit mapper, a
            schedule function, `keys`, `paused`, `restartOn` and `check(s, event)`.
        acceptance
            - A local actor shows each prop's Schema default before any use; `createActor` and `Story.machine` take
              props; `ensureActor`, `actorRef` and `useActorByRef` take none (type tests).
            - A props update is its own turn that changes only props and then runs `onProps`; an equal value is silent;
              it keeps its FIFO place (@spec.Turn.R28, Story tests).
            - The snapshot shows `props`, and every memory reader receives them from the same snapshot.
            - A prop colliding with a memory field, state, trait, operation or emit name is InvalidConfiguration.
            - Local handles have `setProps(partial)` and `createActor` takes props; a ref on a machine with props is a
              type error and refused at construction (R9, R10); `PropWithoutDefault`.
            - Each new rule has a planted fault that `nub run faults` catches.
            - Fixtures first (code:wiki/conventions/fixtures.md): step 5 of code:wiki/conventions/fixture-catalog.md:
              `command-palette`, `search` gain the features, scenarios and sources of their rows; each scenario red
              before the build, and the step's cells marked used at the close.
        comment      2026-11-16T15:38:52Z
            Fixtures first (the user, 2026-11-16): local actors through createActor with props get a fixture first; its
            Stories are this Task's first requirements.

    T77
        title        Declared emits, heard by actor.onEmit and in Stories
        status       draft
        priority     high
        blocked_by   [@T57]
        derives_from [@plan.Machines.O2]
        satisfies    [@plan.Machines.O1, @plan.Machines.O2, @plan.Runtime.O3, @plan.Testing.O1]
        description
            Build declared `emits`, the transition part `emit`, `actor.onEmit`, and emit records in Stories
            and evidence.

            **Why now:** view commands that depend on the cause (focus after Escape, the first invalid field on every
            failed submit) are stored today as a request plus a counter that replays after a reload; the user accepted
            emits (decisions Q3, §9 R6 and the Emits batch). Starts after the Plan merge.

            **Read first:** @spec.Turn.R29, @plan.Machines.O2, code:wiki/conventions/view-commands.md,
            code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/ui-primitives-decisions-2026-11-16.md (§9).

            **Risk:** fragment emits are addressed by instance path and .each key, which need @task.V3.T57's fragments;
            that is the blocker. React delivery (`useOnEmit`) is @task.V3.T30's.

            **Approach:** the payload is computed in the walk (a pre-commit defect) and delivered after the turn
            publishes, in turn order; nothing is stored or replayed.

            May change: `src/`, `fixtures/`, `tests/`. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: an emit asserted only through a listener → caught by emit records in a Story run with no
            listener, and by a reload that replays none.
        acceptance
            - Any accepted transition may emit (send, firing, ending, `onContext` or `onProps` event); entry, exit and a
              refused turn never do; a copy that ends in the same turn still delivers (@spec.Turn.R29).
            - An emit part naming an undeclared emit is a construction error; `UnusedEmit` warning.
            - `actor.onEmit(handlers)` hears emits after their turn, in order; it is not a use; with no listener they
              are dropped; a reload or restore replays none.
            - A fragment's emits are heard under its instance name and an .each copy's key, and appear in its
              summary type.
            - An `emit` evidence kind and Story emit records exist; each new rule has a planted fault.
            - Fixtures first (code:wiki/conventions/fixtures.md): step 3 of code:wiki/conventions/fixture-catalog.md:
              `sign-in`, `command-palette` gain the features, scenarios and sources of their rows; each scenario red
              before the build, and the step's cells marked used at the close.

    T78
        title       Number input on declared props and emits
        status      draft
        priority    medium
        blocked_by  [@T76, @T77]
        description
            Move the done number-input fixture (@task.V3.T71) from `Configure` and `lastCommit` to declared props and
            an emit.

            **Why now:** decisions W8e and §9 R7. The fixture mirrors seven props into memory, re-sends `Configure`
            after every reload, and stores `lastCommit { seq, value }` only to defeat equality.

            **Read first:** code:wiki/conventions/controlled-values.md, code:wiki/conventions/where-a-value-lives.md,
            code:packages/flow-state-v3/fixtures/number-input/machines/stepper.ts, @task.V3.T71's mapping comment.

            **Risk:** a controlled value's emit carries the intent (`Change { by }`), not an absolute value (R7). The
            typed text becomes view-owned, so the display-text scenarios move to view tests (@task.V3.T30) or are listed
            as dropped. The command palette keeps `Configure` (R12).

            **Approach:** drop `min`, `max`, `step` and `wheel` from memory (props), `text` (view) and `lastCommit`
            (emit); write `hold: { direction, size }` once on `Press`; keep T71's mapping, updating each changed case.

            May change: `fixtures/number-input/`, `tests/`. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a migrated case that asserts less than before → caught by re-checking T71's mapping comment case
            by case.
        acceptance
            - The number input declares props and a `Change` emit and has no `Configure` event or `lastCommit` field;
              its Stories set props with the `props(values)` step and assert emit records.
            - Every case in T71's mapping still maps to a test asserting the same behaviour, or is listed as moved to
              view tests or dropped, with its reason.
            - `nub run check`, `nub run test` and `nub run faults` pass.
            - Fixtures first (code:wiki/conventions/fixtures.md): step 6 of code:wiki/conventions/fixture-catalog.md:
              `number-input` gain the features, scenarios and sources of their rows; each scenario red before the build,
              and the step's cells marked used at the close.

    T92
        title       Records decoded into meaning: branded identifiers, each fact stored once, full record decoding
        status      draft
        priority    high
        description
            Saved records decode through Schemas built from the compiled app, with branded identifiers and each fact
            stored once, so the boot never acts on a record nobody checked.

            **Why now:** @task.V3.T20 hit the failure limit on one class: restore trusted a part of a record nobody
            checked; four reviews each found one more (a `~` name, a key naming another identity, lane keys, context
            bindings). The record Schemas in code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/record.ts decode shape only
            (`machine`, `name`, `operation`, `lane` are `Schema.String`), meaning is then checked by hand, and identity
            is stored in both key and content. T20's bindings blocker is parked here as an inverted Restore R5 test. It
            must land before @task.V3.T56 (versions these records) and real storage (@task.V3.T58, @task.V3.T28).

            **Read first:** @spec.Restore (R2, R5), @spec.Codec, @task.V3.T20 (its 2026-11-18T06:49 field list and 07:05
            review), code:wiki/conventions/boundaries.md.

            **Risk:** a Codec and Restore Spec change, so it gets the whole-system check; the format is internal and
            unreleased, so nothing migrates.

            **Approach:** first, decide with evidence whether an actor record's context bindings can be derived from its
            machine and its ref's input; if so, stop saving them. Then:

            - branded identifiers, each a Schema built from Compiled with `Schema.brand`: `ActorName` (a declared,
              persisted machine and a name a ref could give, never `~`), `RecordKey` (`kind/machine/name`, decoded into
              an `ActorName`), `LaneKey` (operation declared as a write, key text as the operation's key fields written
              back exactly), `AttemptId`, `StateName` (a literal union per machine);
            - each fact stored once: identity only in the key; a lane's operation and key text only in its `LaneKey`;
              owed rows name their lane by `LaneKey`; a headless lane stores no `queue` or `inFlight`;
            - full record decoding: one Schema per record kind (actor, lane by operation kind, owed) over those brands,
              through the walker; every hand check in `record.ts` and `actor-record.ts` after decode deleted;
            - the proof: a generated test that corrupts one field of real saved records at a time (from the fixtures'
              Stories), needing no list of fields.

            No real blocker on T81 or the boundary Task: the boot decode is internal and already uses the walker.

            May change: `packages/flow-state-v3/src/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, @spec.Restore, @spec.Codec with
            `specs.log` notes. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a decode that still accepts a field by hand check → caught by the corrupt-one-field test, which
            needs no list of fields. Gates: `nub run faults --only` on T20's restore faults (`record-key-unchecked`,
            `lane-key-text-unread`, `local-name-restored` and the rest) and the new `binding-unchecked`, each caught by
            its named test. User's read: whether bindings are derived or saved; any record a corrupt field leaves
            provably harmless.
        acceptance
            - Bindings: derived or saved, decided with evidence; if saved, decoded as `ActorName`s.
            - Every place a record names an actor, a lane, an Attempt or a state takes the branded type; `record.ts` has
              no `Schema.String` for an identifier and no hand meaning check after decode.
            - A record's content holds nothing its key determines.
            - The corrupt-one-field test: each corrupt record is quarantined and reported (RecordUndecodable) or shown
              harmless; the boot never fails.
            - T20's inverted Restore R5 bindings test passes; a read-operation lane record is quarantined; a headless
              lane's `queue` and `inFlight` are not read.
            - Restore and Codec Specs updated with `specs.log` notes; `nub run check`, the suite and stress pass; `nub
              run faults --only` catches this Task's planted faults.
        comment     2026-11-18T19:05:43Z
            Routed (daily faults, 2026-11-19): throwing-slot-decode-escapes-boot [Restore R5] is missed by the whole
            suite since T94's catch-alls; land a check that a throwing slot decode at boot ends in quarantine, red on
            the plant, before T92's fix work.

    T95
        title       Callback results checked before they enter a turn
        status      draft
        priority    high
        description
            Every user callback's result is checked before it enters a turn: an `updateMemory` patch by its keys and
            each field's Schema, an ending mapper's payload by its event's check.

            **Why now:** found while drafting the boundary map: `updateMemory`'s patch is spread outside its `call`
            (code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/turn.ts#accept), so its keys and values are trusted, and an ending
            mapper's payload enters the turn unchecked against its event
            (code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/core/serve.ts). It is the "user callback results" row of the boundary map,
            the one source with no owner.

            **Read first:** code:wiki/conventions/boundaries.md, @spec.Turn, @spec.Diagnostics, @spec.Codec (R6).

            **Risk:** checking patch values walks large patches twice: the palette's `Configure` patches 100,000
            commands. The bench decides whether production checks values or only keys (dev and test always check both).

            **Approach:** red first, a Story per gap (an unknown patch key, a patch value its field's Schema refuses, a
            mapper payload its event refuses); then one callback wrapper keeping the cause and slot replaces `call`
            (core/turn.ts) and `callSlot` (core/compiled.ts), with the result check inside it.

            May change: `src/core/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, @spec.Turn and @spec.Diagnostics with `specs.log` notes.
            Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a check that runs in dev but not in the host the Story uses → caught by the Story asserting the
            refusal under the stress profile. Gates: `nub run faults --only` on its planted faults `patch-unchecked` and
            `mapper-payload-unchecked`, each caught by its named test; the palette bench, `nub run bench --only
            scale.command-palette.list-length-100000.per-turn-ms --dev`, before and after. User's read: whether
            production checks patch values, from the bench.
        acceptance
            - An unknown patch key, a patch value its field's Schema refuses and a mapper payload its event refuses each
              refuse the turn with a FlowError naming the slot, shown red first by a Story test.
            - One callback wrapper keeps the thrown cause and slot; `call` and `callSlot` are gone.
            - The palette's per-turn median grows by at most 10 % with checks on, or production checks keys only,
              recorded with the bench numbers.
            - `nub run check`, the suite and stress pass; `nub run faults --only` catches this Task's planted faults.

    T96
        title       Actor-to-actor send(ref, event) action with the loop guard
        status      draft
        priority    high
        blocked_by  [@T95]

        satisfies   [@plan.Runtime.O4]
        description
            A machine sends a public event to another actor with a `send(toRef, toEvent)` action, delivered as a later
            turn of the target, with the loop guard that bounds a chain of sends.

            **Why now:** the user's decision (2026-11-18, re-plan Q4 A). Host code is the only way one actor reaches
            another today, which Stories and `flow check` can't see; step-1 fixture cells in pay, sync, transfers,
            palette and player wait on it. The Plans already specify it: @plan.Runtime.O4 (the send table by target),
            @plan.LazyFeatures (a send never adopts), Actors R13 and I20 (the loop guard, built with the action).

            **Read first:** @plan.Runtime.O4, @spec.Actors (R13, I20),
            code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/replan-2026-11-18.md (section
            7), code:wiki/conventions/boundaries.md.

            **Risk:** two machines that send to each other import each other's Machine for the ref; this Task checks
            whether a ref by definition is needed, and that is a public API call for the user.

            **Approach:** `send` is a builder: it captures its two callbacks unread; `machine()` decodes it as an
            action. At run time `toRef`'s result decodes as a ref and `toEvent`'s through the target's event check, both
            in the callback wrapper. Delivery follows O4's table: never creates, never adopts, may restore. The guard:
            past `RuntimeDefaults.loopGuard` events sent by turns from one admitted event, the turn that would send one
            more is refused `InvalidTurn` naming the chain; context updates never count (D13). Listener-originated
            chains (Actors Q4) stay as they are.

            May change: `src/`, `tests/`, `fixtures/`, @spec.Actors with a `specs.log` note. Evidence: this
            Task's comments.

            False pass: a guard that counts but never refuses → caught by a ping-pong Story past the guard that must end
            refused. Gates: `nub run faults --only` on its planted faults `send-creates-target`, `send-adopts-lazy` and
            `loop-guard-off`, each caught by its named test. Stops: a public API change beyond `send(toRef, toEvent)` (a
            ref by definition). User's read: the send's final signature.
        acceptance
            - `send(toRef, toEvent)` delivers by every row of @plan.Runtime.O4's send table, each row a Story in
              a fixture.
            - The waiting step-1 cells in pay, sync, transfers, palette and player use it and pass.
            - A chain past `RuntimeDefaults.loopGuard` is refused `InvalidTurn` naming the chain; a context update chain
              is not.
            - A send's target ref and event are checked; a bad one refuses the sender's turn with a FlowError.
            - `nub run check`, the suite and stress pass; `nub run faults --only` catches this Task's planted faults.
        comment     2026-11-18T19:06:48Z
            Decision (the user, 2026-11-19): send(ref, event) stays as accepted (re-plan Q4 A), as in XState's sendTo,
            framed as a rare escape hatch: docs and examples prefer context, entities and view routing first. Reverse:
            re-plan section 7's B or C.
    T97
        title       Host tests on Story and Fake; the seam harness deleted
        status      draft
        priority    medium
        description
            Flow's own host tests run on Story and Fake only, and `src/host/testing/seam.ts` is deleted.

            **Why now:** moved from @task.V3.T80 in P3 (the user, 2026-11-18, re-plan Q2 B) so P3 closes on
            construction. seam drives the Runtime directly for four host test files (about 2,000 lines) beside Story and
            Fake, with its own store, host and seed copies, against the Epic's "one runtime, one way to run it". T80's
            driver item (Actors R25) moved to the boundary Task.

            **Read first:** @task.V3.T80 (its routed items, in its comments),
            code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/code-quality-review-2026-11-16.md (section 4),
            code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/code-quality-review-2-2026-11-16.md (section 5,
            A7), code:packages/flow-state-v3/src/host/testing/seam.ts.

            **Risk:** coverage must not drop: port one file at a time, keep every rule each test cites, and keep each
            planted fault caught by its target.

            **Approach:** the routed items, all test tooling, land with the port: the pair sweeps stop once every
            reachable phase is found; lanes and player join the reload sweeps; a coverage test that each public feature
            appears in a fixture; edge values in every fixture's step samples; `Failure.shrinks` counts what its doc
            says; failure words and write salts stop leaking across dropped fixtures; generate.ts's placeholders (2g)
            and sweep.ts's booleans (2h); Fake's `Batch` stores no derived status (2i); the test clock's settle is not
            quadratic (3g); `buildRuntime` split once the test-only `admit` hook is gone (5f, widening May change
            to `src/core`).

            May change: `src/host/testing/`, `src/host/*.test.ts`, `src/core/` (5f only), `tests/`. Evidence: this
            Task's comments.

            False pass: a ported test that no longer reaches the rule it cites → caught by `nub run faults --only` on
            each ported file's faults, before and after, with the same faults caught.
        acceptance
            - `lanes.test.ts`, `ledger.test.ts`, `life.test.ts` and `identity.test.ts` run as Story tests over Fake,
              each citing the same rules; `seam.ts` and its copies of the store, host and seed are gone.
            - `nub run faults --only` catches the same faults before and after, each by its own target; the suite and
              stress pass.
            - Fixtures first (code:wiki/conventions/fixtures.md): each ported host test lands in a fixture of
              code:wiki/conventions/fixture-catalog.md as a Story or sample, not as a hand test, and was red on its
              planted fault before the seam file is deleted.
            - The routed items in the Approach are each done or routed on with a reason in this Task's comments.
